### What is the Gregorian leap year rule?

A year is a leap year when:
1. It is divisible by 4, AND
2. It is NOT divisible by 100, UNLESS
3. It is also divisible by 400.

As one boolean: `(year % 4 == 0 && year % 100 != 0) || (year % 400 == 0)`.

### How do you implement it?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.time.Year;

public class LeapYearService {

    public static boolean isLeapYear(int year) {
        return (year % 4 == 0 && year % 100 != 0) || (year % 400 == 0);
    }

    public static boolean isLeapYearStdLib(int year) {
        return Year.of(year).isLeap();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] years = {1900, 2000, 2024, 2023, 1600};
        for (int y : years) {
            System.out.println(y + " -> " + (isLeapYear(y) ? "Leap" : "Not Leap")
                + " (stdlib: " + isLeapYearStdLib(y) + ")");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
1900 -> Not Leap (stdlib: false)
2000 -> Leap (stdlib: true)
2024 -> Leap (stdlib: true)
2023 -> Not Leap (stdlib: false)
1600 -> Leap (stdlib: true)
```

### Why is the century rule a trap?

Most people remember "divisible by 4" but forget the century exception. 1900 is divisible by 4, but it is also divisible by 100 and NOT by 400, so it is not a leap year. 2000 is divisible by 400, so it is a leap year. Getting this wrong is the classic bug.

### Why use Year.isLeap() in production?

Date rules are notoriously easy to get subtly wrong. The standard library implementation is battle-tested and handles edge cases. Writing the explicit condition is fine for learning, but in production code that touches money, delegate to the standard library.

### Why does this matter for banking?

Under an actual/actual day-count convention, the denominator for annualizing interest depends on whether the year is a leap year. A leap year uses 366 days, a normal year 365. Getting this wrong misprices interest.

```java
// Interest calculation
int daysInYear = isLeapYear(year) ? 366 : 365;
BigDecimal dailyRate = annualRate.divide(
    new BigDecimal(daysInYear), 10, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN
);
```

### What are edge cases?

- Year 0: technically leap by the mathematical rule (divisible by 400).
- Negative years (BCE): the modulo rule still works.
- Year 100: not leap (divisible by 100, not by 400).

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// Wrong: only checking divisible by 4
return year % 4 == 0; // says 1900 is leap (WRONG)

// Wrong: wrong operator precedence
return year % 4 == 0 && year % 100 != 0 || year % 400 == 0;
// Without parentheses, && binds tighter, but it still works by chance
// Always use explicit parentheses for clarity!

// Correct: explicit parentheses
return (year % 4 == 0 && year % 100 != 0) || (year % 400 == 0);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Different products use different day-count conventions. The 30/360 convention ignores leap years entirely. The actual/365 convention always uses 365 regardless. Only the actual/actual convention needs leap-year detection. A senior engineer keeps the day-count logic explicit and well-tested, and knows which convention each product uses.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How many leap years are there between year A and year B?
- What day-count conventions exist in banking?
- How does java.time.Year.isLeap() handle negative years?
